:: StoryTitle
Rummy at Ashby

:: StoryData
{
  "ifid": "<generated by Twine>",
  "format": "Inkjot",
  "format-version": "0.6.0",
  "start": "ferry"
}

:: Inklink [config]
(((
A card game inside a story: a sketch for v0.6's decks and piles. Nell is
running from the bailiffs with her grandmother's ghost for company, and the
ferryman will only take a stranger across for a win at rummy.

What it tries: `deck`, `pile`, `meld`, card effects (`shuffle`, `deal`,
`draw`, `lay down`, `discard`, `turn up`), Links made one per thing with
`~ for each`, piles only their owner can see, and an opponent who plays by
a few header effects. None of it is in v0.5.
)))
kind: story-game
mode: solo
pov: nell
character nell: "Nell Ashdown" ~ playable
npc tobin: "Old Tobin, the ferryman" ~ in yard
npc gran: "Gran’s ghost" ~ in yard ~ unseen in sun
place yard: "the yard of the Ferry Inn"

state ferry_paid: no
state peeked: no

(((
A standard deck: 52 cards, no jokers. Each card is a thing with a `rank`,
a `suit` and a `value` (ace 1, court cards 10), shown as "the 7 of hearts".
)))
deck standard ~ ranks ace, 2..=10, jack, queen, king ~ suits spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs ~ jokers 0
~ value ace 1, jack 10, queen 10, king 10
~ shown as "the {rank} of {suit}"

pile stock: standard ~ face down ~ stack
pile discard ~ face up ~ stack
pile hand ~ per player ~ private ~ shown as "hand"
pile laid ~ per player ~ face up ~ shown as "laid down"

(((
Melds. A set is three or four of one rank; a run is three or more of one
suit in sequence, ace low. `melds(pile)` lists every meld the pile holds.
)))
meld set: 3..=4 ~ same rank
meld run: 3.. ~ same suit ~ in sequence ~ ace low

:: ferry
Ashby is a ferry, an inn, and a great deal of brown river. The ferryman sits at a table in the inn yard in full sun, shuffling a pack of cards so old that the kings have no faces left.

“Two of you?” he says, looking at Nell, and then, puzzled, at the empty air beside her, where an old woman’s voice has just said *good afternoon*. “I don’t take strangers over. Not this week. Bailiffs came through asking after a girl.” He squares the deck. “But I’m a sporting man. One hand of rummy. You win, I row you over and I never saw you. I win, you sit in my kitchen till the bailiffs come back.”

> [[Sit down and deal->rummy]] ~ id sit_down
~ choice proactive ~ weighs truth ~ stakes self ~ predict 90%

:: rummy [game]
(((
The game's own header sets the table. `players` names who sits at it, in
order; the pov goes first. Effects in a header run once, on arrival.
)))
players: nell, tobin
shuffle stock
deal 7 from stock to each hand
turn up top of stock onto discard
--
Tobin deals seven each with a flick of the wrist, and turns up {top of discard} beside the stock.
> [[Look at your hand->rummy:turn]] ~ id first_look ~ choice inactive

:: rummy:turn [act]
(((
Your turn: draw, then lay down what you can, then discard one.
)))
--
Your hand: {list my hand}.
Old Tobin holds {count tobin.hand} cards.

[if count tobin.laid > 0; append]
He has laid down {list tobin.laid}.

[if peeked]
Gran’s voice, very low, at your ear: “He holds {list tobin.hand}, dear.”

[end]
> [[Draw from the stock->rummy:drawn]] ~ draw top of stock into my hand ~ id draw_stock
~ choice inactive
> [[Take {top of discard} from the discard->rummy:drawn]] ~ draw top of discard into my hand ~ id draw_discard
~ when count discard > 0
~ choice proactive
> [[“Gran. Go round behind him.”->rummy:peek]] ~ id cheat
~ when not peeked
~ choice proactive ~ weighs truth ~ arc nell falls ~ stakes self ~ predict 50%

:: rummy:peek [act]
set peeked: yes
--
Nobody sees Gran drift round the table. Nobody ever sees her, in the daytime.

Old Tobin frowns, and scratches the back of his neck, as if a draught had touched it.

:: rummy:drawn [act]
--
You draw {last drawn}.
> [[Go on->rummy:play]] ~ id after_drawing ~ choice inactive

:: rummy:play [act]
(((
One Link per meld you could lay down, and one per card you could
discard: `~ for each` makes them. A meld laid down leaves the hand.
)))
--
Your hand: {list my hand}.
> [[Lay down {m}->rummy:play]] ~ for each m in melds(my hand) ~ lay down m from my hand into my laid ~ id lay_down
~ choice proactive
> [[Discard {c}->rummy:tobin]] ~ for each c in my hand ~ discard c from my hand onto discard ~ id discard
~ choice inactive

:: rummy:tobin [act]
(((
Tobin's turn, as plain header effects run in order: take the discard if
it makes him a meld, else draw blind; lay down every meld he holds; throw
away the card worth most that belongs to no meld. A rule of thumb, as an
old man playing for soup would use. Then the game checks for a winner.
)))
let helps: count melds(tobin.hand + top of discard) > count melds(tobin.hand)
let offered: top of discard
draw top of discard into tobin.hand when helps
draw top of stock into tobin.hand when not helps
lay down each m in melds(tobin.hand) from tobin.hand into tobin.laid
let thrown: highest(tobin.hand, by: value, unmelded)
discard thrown from tobin.hand onto discard
next: rummy:out when count my hand == 0 or count tobin.hand == 0
next: rummy:turn
--
[if helps]
Tobin takes {offered} off the discard with a grunt of satisfaction, and throws away {thrown}.

[else]
Tobin draws from the stock, looks at it for a long time, and throws away {thrown}.

:: rummy:out [act]
set ferry_paid: yes when count my hand == 0
--
[if count my hand == 0]
You lay down your last card. Old Tobin looks at the table for a long moment, then laughs, and slaps it with his flat hand.

“Well,” he says. “I’ve been beaten by a girl and a draught. I’ll fetch the oars.”

[else]
Tobin lays down the last of his hand with a little flourish. “Rummy,” he says, kindly. “Kitchen’s through there. There’s soup.”

[end]
> [[Cross the river->across]] ~ when ferry_paid ~ id cross ~ choice inactive
> [[Go into the kitchen->kitchen]] ~ when not ferry_paid ~ id kitchen ~ choice inactive

:: across [ending]
The river is wide and brown and slow, and halfway over, Nell starts laughing and can’t stop.

[if peeked]
“You cheated an old man out of a ferry ride,” says the empty air in the bow, sounding pleased.

“*You* cheated,” says Nell. “I only asked.”

:: kitchen [ending]
The soup is very good. The bailiffs come back at dusk.
